Subject: [PATCH 09303/11362] Refute the systemic click-loss hypothesis, and
 name where the residual risk actually sits

The discriminating test ran over all seven cached windows. vc33 is a click-only game
whose window is ACTION6 and nothing else, every click preserved; sb26 carries clicks
too; and in all seven, window actions equal full-trajectory actions. The builder filters
nothing. The confound that made the question worth asking -- lp85 being the only click
game in the A/B's four-game set -- is broken by a click-only control that set lacked.

So the question narrows rather than closing. The builder did not drop lp85's clicks, it
recorded a different solve than the registry's: 96 clicks over 8 levels in the registry
against a 5-transition all-RESET trajectory that build_window accepted because its final
transition flips level 0 to 1.

That moves the residual systemic risk somewhere new and worth stating. If windows are
accepted on a level-flip criterion and a level flip can follow a reset, then the ACCEPT
CRITERION rather than the action encoding is what can admit a degenerate window -- on any
game, not only click-based ones, with lp85 merely where it bit first. Untested, recorded
as open. The discriminating question is whether lp85's L1 genuinely advances on reset
(mechanics, a real finding) or the level counter is misread (a builder defect with
corpus-wide reach).

The two-layer discriminator needs no revision: lp85's window is degenerate INPUT rather
than degraded ENCODING, which is precisely what layer 1 catches before induction runs.

